course: medicine
lesson: 13
title: Hay fever, allergies and EpiPens
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An allergy is your immune system making a mistake: it treats something harmless, like pollen or peanuts, as an enemy and releases histamine, which makes you sneeze, itch or swell.
explain: Hay fever is an allergy to pollen from grass, trees or weeds, and you cannot catch it from anyone.
explain: A food allergy is not the same as not liking a food: even a tiny bit can cause hives, swelling, sickness or trouble breathing.
explain: The worst reaction, anaphylaxis, can be life-threatening; an adrenaline pen like an EpiPen opens the airways fast, and if in doubt it is safer to use it than to wait.
explain: Eczema, food allergy, hay fever and asthma often come together in the same child, because the immune system is extra sensitive.
fact: Hay fever is an allergy to pollen; histamine released by the immune system causes sneezing, itching and a runny nose, and it is worse on dry, windy days when more pollen is blown about.
fact: Antihistamine tablets, nasal sprays or eye drops help hay fever, and you cannot catch it from someone else.
fact: A food allergy is the immune system overreacting to a food, and even a tiny amount can cause a reaction.
fact: An adrenaline auto-injector opens the airways and raises blood pressure fast; it is pressed firmly into the outer thigh, even through clothes, and 999 is called because the reaction can come back.
fact: Using an adrenaline pen when not sure is safer than waiting.
fact: People with severe allergies carry two in-date adrenaline pens, because one dose may not be enough or one pen may fail.
fact: A rash after a medicine is not always a lifelong allergy; sometimes the rash came from the illness.
fact: Hives are raised itchy red patches caused by histamine, from food, heat, infection or nothing obvious; swelling of lips or tongue or trouble breathing needs an ambulance.
fact: In a skin prick test, tiny drops of possible allergens (things that may cause an allergy) are put on the arm and the skin is gently pricked; it does not give you an allergy.
fact: Many children grow out of milk and egg allergies; peanut and tree nut allergies more often last.
fact: Pet allergies come from proteins in skin flakes and saliva, not just fur; no cat or dog is truly hypoallergenic (free of allergy-causing proteins), though some cause fewer problems.
fact: In the UK food businesses must tell people which of 14 major allergens are in their food; picking the egg out of a dish does not make it safe.
fact: Eczema, food allergy, hay fever and asthma often run together in families and in the same child.
exercise: What is an allergy? | The immune system treating something harmless as an enemy | A germ you catch from friends who sneeze on you in class | A kind of mild food poisoning from old food | Not liking the taste of a food
exercise: What causes hay fever? | An allergy to pollen | A cold virus caught in spring | Hot weather itself, nothing else | Cutting the grass too short
exercise: Can you catch hay fever from a friend? | No, it is not catching | Yes, when they sneeze on you | Yes, by sharing a pencil | Only in the summer holidays
exercise: Why must your friend with a peanut allergy be careful at lunch? | Even a tiny bit of peanut can cause a reaction | Peanuts are poisonous to everyone who eats them | He just doesn't like them | Peanuts make him sleepy in the afternoon
exercise: Where is an EpiPen pressed in? | The outer thigh, even through clothes | Just below the tummy button, on bare skin | The tip of the tongue | The top of the head, under the hair
exercise: What does adrenaline from an EpiPen do? | Opens the airways and raises blood pressure fast | Puts the person to sleep so they calm down quickly | Stops the itching only | Makes the allergy go away for ever, so no more pens
exercise: Why carry two adrenaline pens? | One dose may not be enough or one may fail | Two pens look cooler in your bag at school | One of them is for a friend who might need it | The second is a spare for your pet
exercise: You got an itchy rash after antibiotics. Are you allergic for life? | Not always; sometimes the rash is from the illness | Yes, for ever, to that antibiotic and all others like it | Yes, to all medicines | No, rashes never come from medicines of any kind
exercise: What are hives? | Raised itchy red patches caused by histamine | Tiny holes in the skin where the sweat comes out | Bee nests on the skin | A kind of freckle that comes and goes
exercise: What happens in a skin prick test? | Tiny drops are put on the arm and the skin gently pricked | A lot of blood is drained from the arm to look at | You eat lots of the food | Your skin is burnt a little with a hot needle to see what happens
exercise: Which allergies do many children grow out of? | Milk and egg allergies | Peanut allergies, always, by age ten | Pollen allergies, always, by twelve | None at all, they always stay
exercise: What part of a cat causes a cat allergy? | Proteins in skin flakes and saliva | Only the long hairs on its back and tail | The cat's sharp claws | The sound of the cat's meow
exercise: Why can't the cook just pick the egg out of a dish for an allergic child? | Traces of egg are still in the food | Picking it out takes far too long at lunch | Egg is far too hard to see in a sauce | The egg melts back into the hot dish
exercise: Why did the doctor say eczema, hay fever and asthma are linked? | They often come together because the immune system is extra sensitive | One causes the others by touching the skin | They are all caught from pets in the house | They are really all the same illness
exercise: What should an adult do after using an EpiPen? | Call 999 because the reaction can come back | Go home and rest, as the danger is over now | Wait a week to see | Use a second pen on themselves too
test: teacher wasn't sure if it was a bad reaction, so she waited. good call? | No, using the pen when unsure is safer than waiting | Yes, always wait until you're totally sure it's bad | Yes, wait an hour | No, she should give him a peanut to check
test: Max says Ruby's nut allergy is just being picky. What's the truth? | An allergy is the immune system overreacting, not a dislike | He's right, it's just fussiness about food she doesn't fancy | Allergies are made up | Nut allergies only make you sneeze a bit
test: Your friend left his EpiPen at home "just this once". Any problem? | Yes, a reaction could happen anytime | No, reactions only happen at home | No, one day without it doesn't matter | Yes, it will run out faster at home
test: In a film, a hero cures someone's swelling face with a glass of milk. What's better? | Call 999 and use their adrenaline pen | Give them two big glasses of cold milk | Splash cold water on their face | Let them lie down and sleep it off quietly
test: Aunt says her new poodle is hypoallergenic so my allergy will be fine. Right? | No dog is truly hypoallergenic, though some cause fewer problems | Yes, poodles cause no allergies at all | Yes, all small dogs are safe for allergies | No, poodles cause more allergies than any other dog
test: Hives all over after strawberries, so I'm definitely allergic? | Not definitely; hives can have other causes | Yes, always, hives only come from allergies | Yes, to all fruit | No, hives are from hot weather only
test: Will my allergy test give me new allergies? | No, the test doesn't give you an allergy | Yes, you get one new allergy for each drop | Yes, if it goes red | Only to the drops that sting the most
test: What would happen if the school kitchen didn't list the allergens in its food? | Allergic children could eat something dangerous | Nothing at all, because cooking kills all allergens | The food would taste better | The cook would get an allergy herself
test: Tom outgrew his milk allergy. Will his peanut allergy go too? | Maybe not; peanut allergies more often last | Yes, all allergies go away together at once | Yes, next week | No, and the milk allergy will come back too
test: Why does hay fever get worse on dry, windy days in summer? | More pollen is blown around | The sun burns the inside of the nose | Wind carries cold viruses about | Summer air has less oxygen in it
test: Your mate's lips swell after a sandwich and he wheezes. What first? | Call an adult, 999, and use his adrenaline pen | Give him a glass of water and wait to see | Tell him to lie on his back and sleep it off | Make him eat more to test it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an allergy? | An erroneous immune response to a substance which is ordinarily harmless | A contagious infection transmitted by sneezing between persons | A mere dislike of a particular food | A form of food poisoning
exam: Identify the recommended site for the administration of an adrenaline auto-injector. | The outer aspect of the thigh, through clothing if necessary | The abdomen beneath the navel, upon bare skin only | The surface of the tongue | The scalp
exam: It is uncertain whether a child is experiencing anaphylaxis. Select the response consistent with current advice. | Adrenaline is to be administered, this being safer than delay | Treatment is to be withheld pending certainty as to the diagnosis | The child is to be offered food | The child is to be left to sleep undisturbed
exam: State the reason for which persons with severe allergies are advised to carry two auto-injectors. | A single dose may prove insufficient or a device may fail | Two devices are required by the regulations governing school uniform | The second is intended for a companion | One device is to be kept as a souvenir
